Suppose $G \simeq \bigoplus_{i = 1}^{k} \mathbb{Z}/(d_i)$ with 
    $d_1 \neq 0$ and $d_i \mid d_{i + 1}$ for all $i$. Furthermore 
    assume $G$ acts linearly on $\mathbb{A}^2$ and let $H \subset G$ denote the 
    stabilizer group of the generic point. If $k > 2$ then $H \neq 0$. 
    \begin{proof}
    Let $\sigma: G \rightarrow GL(2)$ denote the representation 
    corresponding to the given action. By the previous lemma we know 
    \begin{equation*}
      \sigma \simeq (d_1;f_{1,1},f_{2,1}) \oplus ... \oplus (d_k; f_{1,k}, f_{2,k})
    \end{equation*}
    for some choice of parameters. As we have in fact proven the 
    corresponding stacks are isomorphic we may prove the claim with the 
    latter action. As such we will calculate 
    \begin{equation*}
      (r_1 e_1 + ... + r_k e_k) \cdot (x, y) = (\zeta^{\frac{d_k}{d_1} r_1 p_{1,1} + ... + r_k p_{1,k}} x, \zeta^{\frac{d_k}{d_1} r_1 p_{2,1} + ... + r_k p_{2,k}} y)
    \end{equation*}
    The given element stabilizes $(x,y) \in \mathbb{A}^2$ iff both exponents 
    are zero modulo $d_k$. As such elements of $H$ are in a bijective 
    correspondence with the kernel of 
    \begin{equation*}
      \begin{pmatrix}
        \frac{d_k}{d_1} p_{1,1} & ... & p_{1,k}\\
        \frac{d_k}{d_1} p_{2,1} & ... & p_{2,k}\\ 
      \end{pmatrix}
      \in \text{Hom}_{\mathbb{Z}}(\mathbb{Z}/(d_k)^{\oplus k},\mathbb{Z}/(d_k)^{\oplus 2})
    \end{equation*}
    As $k \geq 3$ by a counting argument the kernel must be nontrivial so 
    that $H \neq 0$.
    \end{proof}

