The unique rigid stack whose grouping is $(g_x,g_y,-c)(-g_x,-g_y,c)$
  corresponds to the $(3;1,1)$ action.
  \begin{proof}
    Clearly the $(3;1,1)$ action gives rise to a rigid stack 
    and has the necessary grouping. As $g_x = g_y$ this is the only 
    possible cyclic action to consider. Now assume $\mathcal{X}$ 
    corresponds to $(3;A,B) \oplus (3;C,D)$ and form the fundamental matrix.
    As $g_x = g_y$ it is given by 
    \begin{equation*}
      M_G = 
      \begin{pmatrix}
        A & C\\ A & C\\
      \end{pmatrix}
    \end{equation*}
    Then clearly $(-C,A)^T \in \ker(M_G)$ for any choice of $A,C$ 
    and thus no doubly generated with a grouping $(g_x,g_y,-c)(-g_x,-g_y,c)$
    can be rigid.
  \end{proof}

