    $2g_x = 0$ implies $C = 0, 2$, and it may be verified $g_y = -c$ implies
    $(2B,2D) = (-A,-C)$. 
    
    Now assume $m = 2$. By the fact that $2B = -A$ (mod 4) we can see $A = 0$ 
    (mod 2) so the fundamental matrix is given by
    \begin{equation*}
      M_G = 
      \begin{pmatrix}
        2A & C\\ 2B & D\\
      \end{pmatrix}
      =
      \begin{pmatrix}
        0 & C\\ 2B & D\\
      \end{pmatrix}
    \end{equation*}
    If $C = 0$ then clearly $M_G$ has a nontrivial kernel so assume $C = 2$.
    Upon doing so, in order for the matrix to have a trivial kernel it is 
    necessary and sufficient that $B,D$ be congruent to $1,3$ (mod 4) and 
    hence the action is of the form $(2; 0,1) \oplus (4; 2,D)$ for $D = 1,3$.
    As $(-2,-3) = (2,1) \in \mu_4^2$ by Lemma \ref{AutoRemoval} 
    we have $(2;0,1) \oplus (4;2,1) \simeq (2;0,1) \oplus (4;2,3)$.

    Now assume $m = 4$. By the equality $(2B,2D) = (-A,-C)$ the 
    fundamental matrix is given by
    \begin{equation*}
      M_G = 
      \begin{pmatrix}
        A & C\\ B & D\\
      \end{pmatrix}
      =
      \begin{pmatrix}
        2B & 2D\\ B & D\\
      \end{pmatrix}
    \end{equation*}
    from which clearly $(1,-2)^T \in \ker(M_G)$ and hence the corresponding 
    cannot be rigid for any choice of $A,B,C,D$.
  \end{proof}

